```json
[
  {
    "stratum": "Strategy / Commitment",
    "text": "I run a 14-person software company that built a consumer budgeting app used by about 40,000 people. We have 5 months of runway left at current burn ($95k/month). Last quarter, three regional credit unions approached us wanting to license our engine as white-label software for their members, offering combined annual contracts worth $600k with three-year terms. Taking this deal means we'd stop consumer marketing entirely, lay off our two growth-marketing hires, and redirect the team to enterprise integration work for the next 8 months. Alternatively, we could raise a $1.2M seed round from two interested angel investors to keep building the consumer product, betting on hitting 150,000 users by next year, which is what our lead investor said he wants to see before a Series A. We can't do both — the credit union deal requires exclusivity in the credit-union vertical for 3 years, which would preclude any B2C-adjacent product overlap our investors would want. The credit union offer expires in three weeks; the angel commitments are informal but the investors say they need an answer within the month. Which direction should we commit to?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Strategy / Commitment",
    "text": "My family has run a 300-acre grain farm for three generations. We currently sell conventional wheat and soy under a five-year supply contract with a regional co-op that guarantees $210/ton regardless of market swings, with two years left on the contract. An organic food distributor has offered us a separate 7-year contract at $340/ton, but only if we complete organic transition now, which requires three years of reduced yield (est. 25% lower per acre) before certification, no synthetic inputs during that period, and forfeiting our current co-op contract since organic and conventional practices can't run on the same fields under the co-op's rules. My father wants to renew the co-op contract for stability since two of our loans are still outstanding and due in four years; my daughter, who's set to take over the farm, wants to lock in the organic contract now since she believes conventional grain prices will keep declining and organic premiums will only grow. We have to tell both parties within six weeks since the organic distributor is finalizing acreage commitments with several farms this season. Which path should we commit the farm to?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Operations / Execution",
    "text": "Our school district board voted last month to offer full-day pre-K in all 9 elementary schools starting next fall, replacing the half-day program we've run for years. We have committed to this already — the budget is approved and the board isn't revisiting the decision. Now I need to figure out how to actually get there. We have classroom space for full-day pre-K in only 4 of the 9 schools right now; the other 5 would need portable classrooms, which take 10-14 weeks to install once ordered, or renovated space, which is slower. We have 22 current pre-K teachers, but full-day format requires 1.5x the staffing ratio, so we need roughly 14 more hires, and our best hiring window before the credentialing deadline closes is the next 10 weeks. Two schools have wait-list demand of over 40 families each; the other seven range from 15-25. We have budget for 6 portable classroom deliveries this fiscal year, not 5, due to a bid we already accepted, plus funding for 10 new teacher positions this year with the rest phased in next year. Given these constraints, how should we sequence the rollout across the 9 schools?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Operations / Execution",
    "text": "Our company has committed to moving production of our line of commercial water pumps from our contract factory in Vietnam to a newly leased plant in Ohio — the lease is signed, and the decision is final given new tariffs that make overseas production unprofitable within 18 months. We now need to work out the timeline. The Ohio facility needs 4 CNC machines installed, each with an 8-week lead time from the manufacturer, but we can only get 2 delivered simultaneously due to the vendor's shipping capacity. We currently have a backlog of 3,200 pump units already ordered by distributors, due for delivery over the next 5 months, all of which are still expected to come from Vietnam. Vietnam production must wind down since our contract there ends in 6 months and won't be renewed. We need to hire and train roughly 30 machine operators domestically; local trade schools say they can supply about 12 trained candidates every 6 weeks. We also need to requalify our product with 2 major distributors who require a sample-and-inspection process taking 3-4 weeks per batch once the new line is running. Given all this, how should we sequence the machine installs, hiring, Vietnam wind-down, and distributor requalification so we don't leave a supply gap?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Brand / Creative",
    "text": "I'm on the board of a 108-year-old community bank with 12 branches across two counties. We're losing younger account holders to fintech apps — our customer data shows the average age of new checking-account openers has risen from 34 to 51 over the past six years, and our own market surveys say people under 40 describe us as 'a place for their parents.' At the same time, our largest depositors, mostly over 60, consistently tell us in surveys that they trust us because we 'still feel like a real bank, not an app,' and several have told our tellers directly that they'd leave if we 'started acting like a tech company.' We're not changing our name or ownership, and we're not chasing a purely digital-only strategy — we still plan to keep all 12 branches open. But we do need to decide how the bank presents itself now: in advertising, our lobby redesign, our website, and how tellers and loan officers are trained to talk about the bank. Our marketing agency has given us two draft directions — one leans on words like 'modern, local, straightforward,' with brighter colors and younger models in ads; the other leans on 'steady, familiar, always here,' with our founding year featured prominently. We have to greenlight one direction this month before the branch renovation contracts are signed. How should the bank present itself now?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Brand / Creative",
    "text": "I direct a children's museum in a mid-size city, currently called 'Little Explorers Discovery Center.' Attendance from our core audience — families with kids under 8 — is steady, but we're trying to add an early-teen program (ages 11-14) this year to capture a market we're currently missing, and our board wants the museum's public identity to feel credible to that age group without alienating current members. Focus groups with 11-14-year-olds said the current name and its cartoon-mascot logo feel 'little kid' and 'babyish' to them, and several said they wouldn't want to be seen there even with a friend. But our major donors, several longtime members, and our biggest single funder — a foundation that specifically funds 'early childhood discovery centers' — have all told us the name and identity are part of why they associate us with our mission, and our funder said a name change could affect how they view continued funding, since their mandate is specifically early childhood. We aren't closing the early-childhood side of the museum or spinning off a new organization — that's been ruled out as too costly to run two brands. We need one identity that can stretch across both audiences without confusing either. What should our name, visual identity, and voice actually be going forward?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Evidence Interpretation",
    "text": "Our e-commerce site sells outdoor gear, and last quarter our conversion rate rose from 2.1% to 2.9% — a jump we were happy to see. But looking back at what changed, we did three things around the same time: we relaunched the site with a simplified checkout flow (fewer form fields, one-page checkout) in week 1 of the quarter, we ran a site-wide 15%-off promotion for the first 6 weeks of the quarter that then ended, and our paid ad spend increased 40% in the same period, bringing in a different mix of traffic sources (more from Instagram, less from Google search). The conversion rate stayed elevated at 2.7% in the final 3 weeks of the quarter, after the promotion had ended but while the new checkout and ad spend changes were still in place. Average order value dropped slightly from $86 to $81 over the quarter. Traffic volume was up 22% overall. We don't have a controlled test — we changed everything at once because of a deadline tied to our new fulfillment partner going live. I need to know, based on this pattern, what the data can and can't tell us about why conversion improved, and whether it's solid enough to justify keeping the new checkout flow as a permanent fixture."
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Evidence Interpretation",
    "text": "Our hospital's 30-day readmission rate for heart-failure patients dropped from 22% to 15% over the past year, and administration wants to credit this to the new post-discharge nurse call-back program we started, which costs about $340,000 a year to run. But looking more closely, two other things changed in the same period: we opened a new outpatient cardiology clinic that gives many of the same patients faster follow-up appointments (within 5 days instead of the previous average of 18 days), and our case mix shifted somewhat — the average patient age in the heart-failure cohort dropped from 74 to 71, and the share of patients with three or more chronic conditions fell from 61% to 52%, according to our coding data. The call-back program reached about 68% of discharged patients; readmission rates for patients who got a call-back were 12%, versus 19% for those who didn't, but the patients who declined or missed the call-back also tended to be the ones with more chronic conditions and less stable housing situations, per the intake notes. We're due to present findings to the hospital board next month, and they'll decide whether to keep funding the program at its current level. I need help understanding what this data actually demonstrates about the call-back program's effect, and whether it's strong enough evidence on its own."
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Product / Service Design",
    "text": "I run a 3-dentist practice, and about 30% of our patients have no dental insurance, which means they often delay care until something becomes urgent and expensive. I want to launch an in-house membership plan for these patients — something like $30-40/month or an annual fee, covering some baseline care, to keep them coming in regularly and smooth out our own revenue instead of relying on unpredictable walk-in emergency work. I don't have insurance-company involvement in mind at all — this would be entirely between us and the patient. I have some ideas but haven't settled on any of them: how many cleanings and exams should be included per year, whether X-rays should be bundled in or extra, whether we offer a discount tier on procedures like fillings and crowns for members, whether family plans should exist, and whether there should be more than one tier, like a basic plan and a premium plan with more inclusions. I also need to think about whether the plan should auto-renew, whether there's a cancellation policy, and what happens if a member needs a procedure that costs more than the plan covers in savings that year. We have capacity for maybe 400-500 total members across our three dentists without needing to hire more staff. What should this membership plan actually include, and how should it be structured into tiers?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Product / Service Design",
    "text": "I own a 6,000-square-foot coworking space with 85 desks, currently all sold under one flat plan: $350/month for unlimited access. Occupancy is inconsistent — some days we're over capacity with people standing in the kitchen area, other days half the desks sit empty, and we have no way to manage this since everyone has the same access rights. I want to introduce tiered membership options to both manage capacity better and capture more revenue from people who want more, like guaranteed desks, meeting room hours, or after-hours access, without pricing out the people who just want occasional part-time use. I know I want at least a lower-cost option for occasional users and a higher-cost option for people who want a dedicated desk, but I haven't worked out how many tiers make sense, what should be included at each level (meeting room credit hours, printing, mail handling, guest passes, 24-hour access versus business-hours-only), or how to price them so the occasional-use tier doesn't just cannibalize the full-price members we already have. We also have 6 private offices within the space that currently rent separately and could potentially be folded into a top tier. What should our membership tiers actually look like, in terms of what's included and how they're structured?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Finance / Resource Allocation",
    "text": "Our small city council has $2.4 million in discretionary capital funds available this year, and three projects are competing for it, each fully costed and none partially fundable in a way that achieves its purpose. First, resurfacing Route 9's two-mile stretch through downtown, which has deteriorated to the point where our public works director says it will need emergency patching within the year regardless, costing $2.4 million now versus an estimated $3.6 million in three years if deferred. Second, replacing the aging water main under the same downtown stretch, estimated at $1.9 million, which has had 4 breaks in the last two years and serves 1,100 households — public works says a fifth break is likely within 18 months and would cost about $400,000 to fix reactively plus service disruption. Third, building a new $1.8 million playground and splash pad at our largest park, which was promised to residents two years ago after the old equipment was condemned and removed; residents have been vocal at the last three council meetings about the delay. We can fully fund one project, or partially fund two by trimming scope, but not all three even at reduced scope. The city has no additional discretionary capital available until next year's budget cycle. How should we divide the $2.4 million?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Finance / Resource Allocation",
    "text": "My startup has 40 engineers total, and I need to decide how to allocate 6 new engineering headcount slots approved in this year's budget across three teams, since I can't hire more than that until next year's budget cycle. The platform team, which maintains our core infrastructure and is currently 12 people, says they're understaffed relative to on-call burden — they've had 3 people burn out and leave in the last 18 months, and their two remaining senior engineers say they're close to leaving too without relief. The growth team, currently 10 people, is behind on a set of experiments tied to this year's revenue targets, and their lead says each additional engineer could plausibly add about $400k in annual recurring revenue based on current experiment throughput, though that's an estimate, not a guarantee. The new markets team, currently 8 people, is building a product for international expansion that the board has flagged as a priority for next year's fundraising story, but it's currently 3 months behind its original roadmap and needs more hands to hit a demo deadline in 10 weeks tied to investor conversations. I need to divide the 6 new hires across these three teams in a way I can defend to all three leads and to the board. How should I split the headcount?"
  }
]
```
